Background and setup. Over the rationals, the Grothendieck–Ihara program studies the absolute Galois group G_Q = Gal(\bar Q/Q) through its outer action on the étale fundamental group π_1^{ét}(P^1_{\bar Q} \setminus \{0,1,\infty\}). This action produces:
(i) the Ihara power series, a 1-cocycle on G_Q with values in a free pro-l group attached to π_1^{ét}(P^1\setminus\{0,1,\infty\});
(ii) the Deligne–Soulé cocycles, l-adic Galois cocycles refining the cyclotomic character;
(iii) higher circular (cyclotomic) units, which arise as specializations of these cocycles; and
(iv) the multiple zeta values, realized as periods of mixed Tate motives over Z, with the category of mixed Tate motives MT(Z) controlling all of the above through its motivic fundamental group of P^1\setminus\{0,1,\infty\}.

Now let F_q be a finite field, A = F_q[t], K = F_q(t), and K_\infty its completion at the place at infinity. In this function-field setting one has:
- Carlitz–Drinfeld cyclotomic theory with the v-adic Carlitz cyclotomic character \chi_C : Gal(K^{sep}/K) \to A_v^\times;
- function-field analogues of Ihara power series (Anderson's 1-cocycles I_\sigma satisfying I_{\sigma\tau}(Z) = I_\tau(C_{\chi_C(\sigma)}(Z))\, I_\sigma(Z));
- function-field Deligne–Soulé-type cocycles and higher cyclotomic (Carlitz) units;
- Thakur's multiple zeta values \zeta_A(s_1,\dots,s_d) for A = F_q[t]; and
- a theory of mixed Carlitz–Tate t-motives that provides a period interpretation of these multizeta values via iterated extensions of tensor powers of the Carlitz motive.

Problem. Construct an algebraic/geometric object X over K (analogous to P^1_Q\setminus\{0,1,\infty\}) together with an appropriate notion of fundamental group \Pi(X) — playing the role of the étale or motivic fundamental group — such that:

(a) the outer Galois action of Gal(K^{sep}/K) on \Pi(X) produces the Anderson–Ihara power series and the function-field Deligne–Soulé cocycles as canonical 1-cocycles;
(b) the higher Carlitz cyclotomic units arise as specializations of these cocycles;
(c) the periods of \Pi(X), through a Tannakian / motivic realization compatible with the category of mixed Carlitz–Tate t-motives, yield exactly Thakur's multizeta values \zeta_A(s_1,\dots,s_d) and account for their known relations;
(d) the construction parallels, structurally, the way MT(Z) and \pi_1^{ét}(P^1\setminus\{0,1,\infty\}) jointly control Ihara power series, Deligne–Soulé cocycles, cyclotomic units, and classical multiple zeta values.

Identify such an object X and fundamental-group framework, or prove that none exists with the stated properties.

